insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 1, 'A well produces far below the reservoir map because mud filtrate, fines and swollen clays have ruined the first metre of rock. Which route answers that?', '["A matrix treatment, pumping a reactive fluid below fracture pressure so it enters the plugged pore network.", "A hydraulic fracture, because the well is underperforming.", "A hydraulic fracture, since splitting the rock and holding it open with proppant is the only dependable way to lift any weak producer.", "Neither route, because near wellbore plugging always cleans itself out once the well has flowed for long enough."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine calls this a manufactured skin, and in principle every unit of it can be handed back.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 2, 'What physical condition does hydraulic fracturing exist to answer?', '["Perforation tunnels that were shot at the wrong phasing.", "Rock that was never going to flow, because low permeability is a property of every cubic metre out to the drainage radius.", "Rock that is simply tight, which is a near wellbore condition and therefore reachable by any fluid pumped into the first metre.", "Clay swelling and fines migration in the invaded zone left behind by the drilling and completion fluids."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The published case sits in 1 mD, and 1 mD stays 1 mD whether or not anyone ever drilled through it.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 3, 'In the published sandstone case the permeability contrast is 5 and the damage reaches 0.9 m from a wellbore of radius 0.108 m. What damage skin does that cost?', '["0.391324751", "1.6218604324326575, which is what the same case returns once the treated front has been pushed all the way out to the damaged radius.", "8.481054145", "-1.287406553, the value the published carbonate job returns after wormholes have been cut."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That single number is entirely manufactured by the drilling and completion sequence rather than by the reservoir itself.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 4, 'How far can a sandstone matrix acid job take the damage skin at best?', '["To -1.287406553, matching the published carbonate result.", "To -5.3116380662677045, since a well thoroughly cleaned up in sandstone reaches the same place a propped fracture reaches.", "To a small negative value, because dissolving the plugging material always enlarges the pore throats beyond their original size.", "To exactly 0, which is the well behaving as an undamaged hole of its own radius and no better."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acid in sandstone unwinds a number you created, so the arithmetic stops once the created number is gone.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 5, 'Why can acidising a carbonate return a negative skin when acidising a sandstone cannot?', '["Carbonate acid restores the damaged permeability more completely.", "Wormholes cut channels that reach out past the wellbore, so the well gains a flow path it never had.", "Carbonate rock has a higher initial permeability, which means the same volume of acid penetrates further and removes a greater share of the plugging material.", "Carbonate jobs are pumped above the fracture pressure, so they split the rock as well as dissolving it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The published carbonate job pumps 8 m3 and reaches a wormhole radius of 0.391324751 m.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 6, 'The published fracture with a 150 m half length returns a pseudo skin of -5.3116380662677045. What is the equivalent statement about the wellbore?', '["The well behaves as though the reservoir permeability were higher.", "The well behaves as though the drainage radius had shrunk from 300 m to something far closer in, which is what makes the pressure drop smaller.", "The well behaves as though its effective radius were 21.889652014700083 m rather than 0.108 m.", "The well behaves as though the damaged ring had been dissolved out to 0.9 m and no plugging material remained."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A negative pseudo skin of that size is a different mechanism from damage removal, since nothing has been taken away.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 7, 'Damage and tight rock look identical from surface, both giving a well far below expectation. Which measurement separates them?', '["A pressure transient test, since a large positive skin with reasonable permeability points to damage.", "A production log run across the completed interval.", "A laboratory core flood, because measuring the permeability of a recovered plug is the only way to establish whether the near wellbore rock has been degraded.", "The wellhead pressure, which falls in a damaged well and holds up in a tight one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mirror reading is the diagnostic one: a small skin alongside poor permeability says the rock was always tight.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 8, 'The engine holds nine calculations split into a matrix family and a fracture family. What single quantity do both families produce?', '["A treated radius expressed in metres.", "A pumped volume in cubic metres, which both routes need before anything can be placed and which therefore ties the two families together.", "A production rate, since the point of any stimulation job is more oil per day out of the same reservoir.", "A skin, which is why two routes that share almost no arithmetic can still be compared."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Skin carries no units, so it travels between wells, fields and fluids without conversion.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 9, 'What does the matrix rate ceiling in this engine actually calculate?', '["The highest injection rate that still keeps bottomhole pressure below the fracture pressure.", "The rate at which acid spends completely along the flow path.", "The rate that maximises the depth the treated front reaches, found by balancing the pumped volume against the leakoff into the surrounding formation.", "The rate above which wormholes stop branching and grow as a single dominant channel."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cross that ceiling and the job stops being a matrix job, because the rock splits instead of accepting fluid through its pores.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 10, 'Pushing the treated front to 0.6 m in the published interval takes 29.546905102 m3 of acid. What skin does the case still carry afterwards?', '["0, since any acid placed in the damaged ring removes the damage.", "1.6218604324326575, because the front has stopped short of the 0.9 m damaged radius.", "8.481054145, unchanged, because a treated front that fails to reach the full damaged radius leaves the original damage skin exactly where it was.", "-1.287406553, matching what wormholes buy in the published carbonate case."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reaching the full 0.9 m instead takes 67.71725584279905 m3, and volume grows with the square of the radius.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 11, 'How does this engine represent acid on the sandstone side?', '["As a spending profile along the flow path.", "As a stoichiometric balance between the acid strength and the mineralogy of the formation, so the dissolving power sets how far the front travels.", "Purely as a volume of pore space, with a stated planning rule and no reaction kinetics anywhere.", "As a reaction rate that falls as the acid is consumed, which fixes the live acid penetration depth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published sandstone rule uses a pore volume factor of 1.5 applied to the pore volume of the treated annulus.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 12, 'Where does the pore volumes to breakthrough figure, 1 in the published carbonate case, have to come from?', '["From the measured permeability contrast across the damaged ring.", "From the pumped volume and the wormhole radius, which between them fix how many pore volumes were required to reach breakthrough in that rock.", "From the porosity and the treated interval height, since those two set the pore volume of the annulus.", "From a laboratory core flood that the user supplies, because the engine cannot compute it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It stands in for all of the reaction chemistry the model deliberately leaves out.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 13, 'Which limitation applies to the fracture side of this engine?', '["It cannot compute a fracture width at any half length.", "It is two dimensional, with height fixed as an input at 30 m in the published case.", "It grows the fracture in height as the net pressure rises, but it does so without any proppant transport model, which makes the placement optimistic.", "It requires a reaction kinetics model before the pad volume can be sized."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no proppant settling and no transport either, so the pack lands where the arithmetic says rather than where gravity would put it.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 14, 'The engine throws rather than guess when a permeability contrast below 1 is entered. What is the reason?', '["A ratio under 1 describes near wellbore rock better than the reservoir, which is a stimulated well.", "Logarithms of numbers below 1 cannot be evaluated.", "The published case uses a contrast of 5, and values far away from the published case fall outside the range over which the correlation was fitted.", "A contrast under 1 would make the treated volume negative and the planning arithmetic meaningless."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine also refuses a damaged radius or an acid front placed inside the wellbore radius.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-this-course-decides', 15, 'Which output should nobody expect from this engine?', '["A wormhole radius for a carbonate treatment.", "A pseudo skin for a propped fracture, because turning geometry back into one number is a reservoir engineering step performed elsewhere.", "A production forecast, since the outputs stop at a skin and a geometry.", "A pumped volume for a sandstone job at a chosen front radius."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A skin is a dimensionless statement about pressure drop, and converting it into barrels belongs to a different discipline.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 1, 'Skin is described in this module as a bookkeeping number rather than a thing in the rock. What is it keeping account of?', '["The volume of drilling mud lost into the formation.", "The extra pressure drop the well takes over and above what the drainage logarithm alone predicts.", "The permeability of the damaged ring, expressed relative to a reference value so that wells drilled in different reservoirs can be compared.", "The fraction of the completed interval that is actually open to flow after perforating."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Writing that extra drop as one number saves modelling the damaged ring explicitly on every calculation.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 2, 'Why must skin be a dimensionless quantity?', '["It is defined as a ratio of two permeabilities.", "It is normally reported as a value between about 0 and 10, and a range that narrow only makes sense if the number carries no physical units at all.", "It is added to the drainage logarithm, which is itself dimensionless, so it lands in the same place.", "It is derived from a pressure difference divided by a reference pressure taken at the sandface."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Carrying no units is exactly what lets a skin be compared across wells, fields and fluids.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 3, 'In the published case the damage skin is 8.481054145 while the drainage logarithm for 300 m on a 0.108 m wellbore is smaller. What does that comparison say?', '["The drainage radius has been entered incorrectly.", "The damaged ring and the undamaged reservoir share the pressure drop roughly evenly, which is the usual arrangement in a moderately damaged well.", "The permeability of the reservoir must be poor, since a large skin can only arise in tight rock.", "The last 0.9 m of rock takes more of the drawdown than the whole remaining 299 m of reservoir."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Most of the drop sits near the well in any case, because that is where the flow area is smallest.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 4, 'A well returns a negative skin. What does that require physically?', '["Something has been added to the well, such as wormholes, a fracture or an enlarged effective radius.", "The damaged ring has been dissolved away completely.", "The near wellbore permeability has been restored to the undamaged reservoir value, which is the best outcome any successful treatment can produce.", "The measured drawdown was taken before the well had reached steady radial flow."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither the published carbonate job nor the published fracture is merely a well without damage.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 5, 'Which two factors does the Hawkins formula multiply together?', '["The damaged permeability and the treated annulus volume.", "The contrast less 1, and the logarithm of the damaged radius divided by the wellbore radius.", "The undamaged permeability and the ratio of the drainage radius to the wellbore radius, which between them set how much resistance the near wellbore rock adds.", "The porosity of the damaged ring and the thickness of the completed interval."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hawkins pictures the damage as a clean cylindrical ring with uniform degraded permeability inside it and untouched rock outside.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 6, 'Setting the permeability contrast to 1 drives the Hawkins skin to 0 no matter how thick the damaged ring is. Why does subtracting 1 do that?', '["The logarithm of 1 is 0, which zeroes the product.", "A contrast of 1 sits at the lower limit the engine accepts, and values at a limit are returned as 0 rather than as a computed result.", "A contrast of 1 means the ring is not degraded, so the first factor vanishes and the extent term cannot matter.", "The damaged radius and the wellbore radius become equal whenever the contrast is 1."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is a sanity check worth running in your head before trusting any skin the formula hands you.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 7, 'Hawkins needs no absolute permeability value, only a ratio. What follows from that?', '["The same skin can arise in a 1 mD rock and in a 1000 mD rock, provided the contrast and radii match.", "Damage skin scales directly with reservoir quality.", "The formula can only be trusted in reservoirs where the undamaged permeability has been measured independently, since a ratio on its own is not enough to fix the answer.", "A skin measured in one well can be transferred to any other well drilled in the same field."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Damage skin says how much worse the near wellbore rock is than the rest, and nothing at all about how good the reservoir is.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 8, 'Holding the damaged radius at 0.9 m and the wellbore at 0.108 m, what skin does a permeability contrast of 20 produce?', '["13.296945362104108", "8.481054144800364, since the published case already carries the deepest damage the sweep considers and the skin is capped there.", "2.120263536200091, which is what a single unit of contrast is worth at that radius.", "40.28500718780173"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Severe damage confined to a thin ring is expensive, and that row is the most costly in the contrast sweep.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 9, 'In the radius sweep run at the published contrast, damage extending 3.0 m into the formation returns which value?', '["23.322898898201, taken from the contrast sweep instead.", "13.296945362104108, so mild damage that reaches a long way is also expensive.", "8.481054144800364, unchanged from the published case, because past about 1 m the logarithm has flattened off completely and further reach costs nothing.", "10.524356639864328, which is the value that belongs to a damaged radius of 1.5 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That row and the 0.2 m row differ by a factor of fifteen in reach yet by less than a factor of six in skin.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 10, 'A contrast of 3 at 0.9 m gives 4.240527072400182, and a contrast of 5 at 0.4 m gives 5.23733327993505. What does that pairing demonstrate?', '["The sweep tables contain a transcription error.", "Skin depends only on the product of the contrast and the damaged radius, which is why quite different combinations of the two land in the same place.", "Two completely different wells can carry a similar skin, so one number never fixes the pair behind it.", "A shallow ring is always worse than a deep one once the contrast rises above about 4."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That matters for design, because how far out to push the acid front depends on the radius, which is exactly the number a well test never hands you.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 11, 'Each extra unit added to the permeability contrast adds the same 2.120263536200091 to the skin at the published damaged radius. What kind of relationship is that?', '["A straight line that never flattens, so the hundredth unit of contrast costs exactly what the second one did.", "A logarithmic relationship that flattens as contrast rises.", "A relationship that is linear at low contrast but bends over above a value of about 8, which is where the sweep table starts to show diminishing steps.", "A square law, since the skin roughly quadruples when the contrast doubles."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That fixed cost per unit is what makes an error of one unit in the contrast so damaging to a design.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 12, 'Moving the damaged radius from 0.2 m to 0.4 m raises the skin by the same amount as moving it from 1.5 m to 3.0 m. What explains that?', '["The two steps cover an identical thickness of rock.", "The contrast was changed between the two steps in a way that happens to compensate for the different amounts of rock each one covers.", "The skin becomes insensitive to the damaged radius once that radius exceeds the wellbore radius by a factor of ten.", "Both steps are a doubling, and the logarithm counts only doublings rather than metres of rock."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The second step covers 1.5 m of rock against 0.2 m for the first, and buys precisely the same skin.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 13, 'Given that skin is linear in the contrast and only logarithmic in the radius, where should a diagnostic budget be spent?', '["On the damaged radius, which is the harder number to measure.", "On the contrast, since an error of one unit moves the answer by a fixed and substantial amount.", "On both equally, because the Hawkins formula multiplies the two factors together and is therefore no more sensitive to one of them than to the other.", "On the drainage radius, which sets the logarithm the skin is added to."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An error of a factor of two in the damaged radius moves the answer by less, and by less and less the further out you go.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 14, 'Reaching an acid front at 0.3 m in the published interval takes 6.6446946570134715 m3, while reaching 0.6 m takes 29.546905101683063 m3. What does that contrast show?', '["Acid spends faster the deeper it penetrates the formation.", "The pore volume factor rises with distance from the wellbore, which is why the second front needs a disproportionate share of the total pumped volume.", "Treated volume grows with the square of the radius, so each further doubling of the front costs several times the acid.", "The engine adds a safety margin to any front placed beyond half a metre from the wellbore."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Skin is bought logarithmically while acid is bought by volume, which is the asymmetry that limits how deep a matrix job should aim.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-damage-and-what-it-costs', 15, 'Which effects are folded into the single skin value a pressure transient test reports?', '["Formation damage, perforation geometry, partial penetration, deviation, completion scale and turbulence together.", "Formation damage alone, which is what Hawkins computes.", "Formation damage and the fracture pseudo skin, since a test cannot separate a natural flow path from one that was deliberately created by pumping.", "Formation damage and the drainage logarithm, which the test reports as a combined resistance term."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Deviation works the other way from the rest and contributes a negative term, while turbulence grows with flow rate rather than staying put.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 1, 'In a sandstone treatment, what is the acid there to dissolve?', '["The quartz framework of the reservoir rock itself.", "The carbonate cement that binds the grains together, since hydrochloric acid attacks nothing else present in the interval.", "The material plugging the pore throats, such as mud solids, migrated clay fines and scale.", "Whatever mineral is most abundant in the interval, which the mineralogy report identifies."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hydrofluoric blends can attack quartz, but they are carried to clear the throats rather than to remove reservoir rock.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 2, 'Why can a carbonate job never be designed to dissolve only the damage?', '["The damage and the rock framework are the same chemical target, so acid attacks both at once.", "Carbonate acid is always too weak to distinguish between minerals.", "The damaged zone in a carbonate is normally deeper than any acid volume could reach, so selectivity would be pointless anyway.", "Hydrochloric acid is only ever pumped after the damaged shell has been drilled out mechanically."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hydrochloric acid on limestone attacks the matrix fast and almost completely, which is what makes channelling possible.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 3, 'Which quantity does the sandstone design ask you to choose, and which does the engine return?', '["You choose a pumped volume and the engine returns the front radius.", "You choose an injection rate and the engine returns the time needed to place the treatment at that rate.", "You choose the acid strength and the engine returns the depth of live acid penetration for that strength.", "You choose a target acid front radius and the engine returns the planning volume plus the skin left."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The carbonate routine runs the other way round, taking a pumped volume and reporting how far the channels effectively reached.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 4, 'What does the pore volume of the treated annulus represent?', '["The total bulk volume of rock inside the target radius.", "The fluid needed to fill the pore space between the wellbore and the target radius exactly once.", "The volume of rock that the acid must dissolve before the front can advance any further into the formation.", "The volume of acid that will be spent by reaction before it reaches the outer edge of the damaged zone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Acid travels in pores rather than through grains, which is why that annular pore space anchors the whole calculation.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 5, 'The published sandstone cases apply a pore-volume factor of 1.5. What is that allowance for?', '["The acid strength needed to dissolve the mineral present.", "A structural safety margin that makes the treatment half again as likely to meet its production target.", "Real displacement is not a piston, so acid fingers into the best streaks and leaves tight layers barely touched.", "The stoichiometric excess required because each cubic metre of acid dissolves less than a cubic metre of solids."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Setting the factor to 1 would assert perfect piston displacement of every pore in the annulus, which nobody achieves.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 6, 'Why can an acid strength never be recovered from the pore-volume factor?', '["The factor is fixed at 1.5 and cannot be edited by the user.", "Acid strength is recovered instead from the residual skin, which already contains the dissolving power of the blend.", "The factor is a laboratory measurement whose units cancel, leaving no chemical information behind.", "The engine tracks the front volumetrically, so no reaction chemistry or mineralogy went into the number."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Preflush design and mineralogy are left out of scope entirely, so the factor is a placement allowance and nothing more.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 7, 'Which inputs will the sandstone routine refuse outright?', '["A pore-volume factor that is not positive, and a porosity that is not strictly between 0 and 1.", "Any porosity below 0.18 and any interval shorter than 100 m.", "A permeability contrast above 5, because the Hawkins expression is not valid beyond that contrast.", "A pumped volume smaller than the pore volume of the damaged annulus, which cannot advance a front."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those guards catch nonsense rather than poor design, since a factor of zero would claim the annulus needs no acid at all.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 8, 'Diversion methods such as ball sealers and self diverting acid appear where in the engine?', '["In the pore-volume factor, which is calibrated from ball sealer trials.", "Nowhere, so the returned volume is what a perfectly diverted treatment would require.", "In the residual skin, which rises when the model judges that placement across the interval was uneven.", "In the refusal conditions, which reject any design whose interval is too long to be treated in one stage."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Getting near to perfect diversion is usually more of the design effort than choosing the volume ever was.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 9, 'Which properties play no part in setting the sandstone planning volume?', '["Porosity and the interval height.", "The wellbore radius and the target front radius, both of which affect only the residual skin after treatment.", "The pore-volume factor, which acts on the skin rather than on the volume delivered to the interval.", "Permeability and the damage contrast, since the volume depends only on geometry and porosity."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The damage numbers still matter, but they enter the skin the treatment leaves behind rather than the litres on the ticket.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 10, 'Doubling the target front radius from 0.3 m to 0.6 m has what effect on the acid volume?', '["It doubles the volume, from 6.64 m3 to about 13 m3.", "It leaves the volume unchanged, because the extra reach is paid for by the pore-volume factor rather than by area.", "It costs slightly more than four times as much, because the volume carries the square of the radius.", "It costs about eight times as much, because the treated region grows as the cube of the radius."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The excess over a clean factor of four comes from subtracting the wellbore radius, which weighs more at small radii.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 11, 'Going out from a 0.6 m front to a 0.9 m front changes the job how?', '["The volume rises from 29.55 m3 to about 44 m3.", "The volume rises from 29.55 m3 to 67.72 m3, more than twice the published treatment.", "The volume falls, because the wellbore radius is subtracted from a larger area and matters proportionally less.", "The volume rises to 121.16 m3, which is the figure the table gives for full removal of the damage."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Deep penetration in a sandstone gets dearer the deeper you already are, since each successive step buys more area.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 12, 'The published treatment leaves a residual skin of 1.62. Where does that residual come from?', '["A shell of damaged rock from 0.6 m out to 0.9 m stays at the reduced permeability.", "Reaction products that the acid leaves in the pore throats.", "The pore-volume factor of 1.5, which the engine subtracts from the original damage skin of 8.48.", "Incomplete placement over the 100 m interval, which the engine estimates from the interval height."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The same Hawkins expression is reapplied, with the shell now running from the front radius rather than from the wellbore.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 13, 'When does the engine report the damage as removed?', '["Once the volume pumped exceeds 30 m3 on this well.", "Once the residual skin falls below 1.62, at which point the remaining damage is treated as negligible.", "Only once the acid front radius reaches or passes the damage radius, which here means 0.9 m.", "Progressively, in proportion to the fraction of the damaged annulus the front has swept through."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A job that stops short earns no partial credit in proportion to the money spent, because the flag is a hard condition.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 14, 'Pumping out to a 1.2 m front costs 121.16 m3. What does the extra acid buy in this model?', '["A skin of about -1.29, the same as the carbonate case.", "Nothing, because the skin is already zero at 0.9 m and the reward saturates once the damage has gone.", "A proportionally deeper front, which the model converts into further skin reduction below zero as acid advances.", "A halving of the residual skin for each additional 0.3 m of front radius bought."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Restoring permeability cannot make rock better than it was drilled, so zero is the floor for the sandstone route.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-acid-route', 15, 'A job pumps exactly the designed volume yet production does not improve. What is the likeliest reason?', '["Acid followed the least resistance into clean high permeability rock and the damaged intervals saw little.", "The pore-volume factor was set to 1.5 rather than 1.", "The planning volume was computed from the damage radius instead of from the chosen acid front radius.", "The engine over-predicts the residual skin, so a job reported as poor had in fact removed all of the damage."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A treated sandstone often keeps a small positive skin anyway, from unrecovered fines and reaction products the model ignores.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 1, 'What stops carbonate acid from simply dissolving the rock uniformly around the well?', '["The acid is spent before it can reach the rock face.", "Acid dissolves fastest where flow is fastest, and that feedback runs away into a few dominant channels.", "Hydrochloric acid attacks only the cement between grains, so the framework itself survives the treatment intact.", "Injection is held below the optimum rate so that dissolution stays confined to the immediate wellbore wall."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same runaway feedback would be unwelcome in a sandstone job, where the object is to sweep a front rather than cut channels.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 2, 'Why would uniform dissolution be uneconomic even over a modest enlargement?', '["Uniform fronts move too slowly to reach past the damage.", "Hydrochloric acid loses strength with depth, so a uniform front cannot be maintained beyond the first metre of rock.", "The engine refuses any volume large enough to dissolve a full shell, so the case cannot be designed at all.", "The whole solid volume of the shell would have to go, and acid dissolves only a fraction of its own volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Merely filling the sandstone pore space out to 0.6 m already takes 29.55 m3, and taking the grains as well is a far larger job.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 3, 'How does the engine obtain the wormhole radius?', '["From a volumetric balance, where pumped volume equals the annular pore space times the pore volumes to breakthrough.", "From a channel growth model fitted to core flood photographs.", "From the skin measured on a pressure transient test, which is inverted to give the radius of the treated cylinder.", "From the injection rate and the reaction rate of the acid blend."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Written out, the radius is the square root of the wellbore radius squared plus the pumped volume over pi, height, porosity and the count.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 4, 'In what sense is the carbonate formula the sandstone volume equation read backwards?', '["The porosity enters as its reciprocal rather than directly.", "The interval height is divided out instead of multiplied in, which is what turns a volume into a radius.", "The same balance appears, with pore volumes to breakthrough standing where the pore-volume factor stood.", "The wellbore radius is added to the target radius rather than subtracted, so the annulus becomes a full cylinder."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One volumetric balance therefore serves both routes, and only the direction of the calculation changes between them.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 5, 'Which end of the calculation is held fixed in each of the two routes?', '["Both routes fix a volume and return a skin.", "Sandstone fixes a radius and returns a volume, while carbonate fixes a volume and returns a radius.", "Sandstone fixes the pumped volume and carbonate fixes the front radius, which is why their skins differ in sign.", "Both fix the interval height, and everything else follows from porosity and the pore-volume factor chosen."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That asymmetry follows from the design question, since a sandstone job is sold on reach and a carbonate job on spend.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 6, 'Because the wormhole radius sits under a square root, how does it respond to more acid?', '["It grows as the square root of the volume, so successive doublings multiply it by the square root of two.", "It grows in direct proportion to the volume pumped.", "It rises quickly at first and then falls back once the squared wellbore radius comes to dominate the total.", "It doubles whenever the pore volumes to breakthrough are halved, independently of the volume pumped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The squared wellbore term is a visible share of the total at 2 m3 and is almost lost in it by 32 m3.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 7, 'What does the radius returned by the carbonate routine actually describe?', '["The distance the longest single channel has reached.", "The mean spacing between neighbouring wormholes, averaged over the treated height of the interval.", "The radius at which the acid has been fully spent, beyond which no further dissolution is possible at all.", "The radius of an equivalent fully stimulated cylinder, not the tip of any individual wormhole."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Real channels are sparse and branching with untouched rock between them, and that fiction is what lets a skin be written at all.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 8, 'Where does the pore volumes to breakthrough number come from?', '["A correlation on porosity and interval height.", "The engine derives it from the injection rate, the acid strength and the reservoir temperature that you supply.", "A core flood in the laboratory, counted until a channel breaks through and the pressure drop collapses.", "A pressure transient test run after the treatment, which reports it alongside the post job skin."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is an efficiency measure of how much acid the rock demands per unit wormholed through, so a low count is the good result.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 9, 'At the optimum injection rate, what happens to the breakthrough count?', '["It reaches its minimum, rising again if you pump either slower or faster than that rate.", "It reaches its maximum, which is why the rate is chosen.", "It becomes independent of rate, which is what makes a single laboratory number usable in a field design.", "It falls to 1, the value the published carbonate case uses for this reservoir carbonate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pumping slowly spends acid at the face, while pumping hard outruns the instability, and both waste acid for the same reach.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 10, 'Doubling the pore volumes to breakthrough has the same effect as which change?', '["Doubling the acid pumped, for the same reason.", "Halving the acid pumped, since only the quotient of the two ever enters the calculation.", "Halving the porosity, which the formula treats as interchangeable with the breakthrough count in the balance.", "Doubling the interval height, because both quantities multiply the annular pore space in the denominator."]'::jsonb, 1, 'So 32 m3 at a count of 1 reaches the same 0.76 m as 8 m3 into rock whose count is four times as large.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 11, 'How is the skin written once the wormhole radius is known?', '["As the Hawkins expression with the contrast set to 5.", "As minus the ratio of the two radii, so a wormhole twice the wellbore radius would return a skin of exactly -2.", "As minus the natural logarithm of the wormhole radius divided by the drilled wellbore radius.", "As the logarithm of the pumped volume divided by the pore volume of the treated interval annulus."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is the effective wellbore radius definition run in reverse, with the wormholed cylinder standing as the enlarged well.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 12, 'Reading down the skin column of the doubling table, what pattern appears?', '["A decrement that doubles with each row of the table.", "A decrement that shrinks towards nothing, so the skin approaches a floor of about -2 whatever is pumped.", "A decrement that varies with the interval height, which is why the table is only valid over 100 m of section.", "A nearly constant decrement per doubling, settling towards half the natural logarithm of two."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The radius column tells the same story from the other side, with successive ratios creeping up towards the square root of two.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 13, 'Sixteen times the acid takes the skin from -0.70 to -1.95. What does that show?', '["The skin scales linearly with the volume pumped.", "The bargain is brutal, since the cost of the next unit of skin doubles each time one is claimed.", "The model is unreliable at large volumes, which is why the tabulated sweep stops at 32 m3 of acid.", "Skin and volume are proportional once the squared wellbore radius has become negligible in the balance."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is why carbonate treatments cluster at modest volumes, because the first few cubic metres do most of the work.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 14, 'Why does this model never point to a best carbonate volume?', '["The skin keeps improving, however slowly, so the stopping point has to come from cost outside the model.", "It refuses volumes above 32 m3, so no optimum can be found.", "It reports an optimum injection rate instead, so volume is left to be fixed by the pumping time available on site.", "The optimum lies at the volume where the wormhole radius equals the damage radius, which is not modelled here."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A flattening skin curve meeting a cost curve that has not flattened is where the real stopping point is decided.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-carbonates-and-wormholes', 15, 'The sandstone job ends at a skin of 1.62 and the carbonate job at -1.29. How should that be read?', '["The carbonate route is the better job on any well.", "The two skins can be added to give a single treated skin of 0.33 for a damaged carbonate reservoir.", "The baselines differ, and the sandstone job recovered far more skin than the carbonate job managed.", "The sandstone job failed outright, because any positive skin after treatment means the acid never touched the damage."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine keeps the Hawkins damage skin and the wormhole skin apart on purpose, and they should not be summed.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 1, 'Which single condition separates a matrix acid treatment from an acid fracture treatment?', '["The bottomhole pressure stays below the pressure at which the formation parts, so no new plane opens.", "The acid blend is weaker, so it cannot part the rock.", "The treatment is pumped through coiled tubing rather than down the casing, which limits what the rock sees.", "The pump spread on location is rated below the horsepower that a fracture treatment would need to reach."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Same acid, same well and same pumps can give either job, because the label follows the bottomhole pressure alone.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 2, 'In the published case, what does the engine take as the fracturing pressure for the matrix ceiling?', '["The surface pump pressure recorded during the step rate test.", "The reservoir pressure of 23.73 MPa raised by a fixed safety allowance chosen by the designer on the day.", "The closure stress of the interval, 38.13 MPa, at a mid depth of 2124.897477742 m true vertical.", "The PKN bottomhole treating pressure of 41.02 MPa, since that is the pressure the fracture half of the case pumps at."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Closure belongs to the earth stress, so the ceiling is fixed by the formation rather than by anything on surface.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 3, 'Reading the ceiling equation as a driving force over a resistance, which grouping sits in the numerator?', '["The acid viscosity together with the logarithm of the radius ratio.", "The pressure margin between fracturing and reservoir pressure, multiplied by the permeability thickness product.", "The skin, because extra resistance near the wellbore is what the pumps have to overcome before any acid moves.", "The wellbore radius and the drainage radius, since the geometry of radial flow is what the margin has to push against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same steady radial expression serves production inflow, run here with the pressures the other way round.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 4, 'At what skin is the published ceiling of 0.000544181 m3/s quoted?', '["At zero skin, which is the clean well condition.", "At the skin left after the acid has finished, so the number describes the well an operator hands back.", "At an average of the skin before and after treatment, so that the quoted duration covers the whole pumping period.", "At the damaged skin of 8.481054145, the Hawkins value for a permeability ratio of 5 out to 0.9 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Quoting the ceiling before any acid is deliberate, because that is the slowest the well will ever accept fluid.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 5, 'Why does acid pumped above the fracturing pressure treat the damage poorly?', '["It reacts too quickly at the higher pressure.", "It cools the near wellbore rock enough to raise the acid viscosity, so the reaction front stalls before it reaches the damage.", "It runs a long way along one plane and leaves most of the damaged ring around the wellbore untouched.", "It dissolves the cement sheath in preference to the formation, because the fracture face exposes the annulus to live acid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Control of placement goes as well, since a fracture can grow out of the target sand into a shale or into water.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 6, 'What does the rate model do when the fracturing pressure does not exceed the reservoir pressure?', '["It refuses to run and says so, because with no pressure margin there is no injection to compute at all.", "It returns a rate of zero without comment.", "It substitutes a default margin of one tenth of the closure stress so that a screening number can still be produced.", "It reverses the sign of the margin and reports the resulting production rate, leaving the user to notice the change."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Returning zero would look like a computed answer, so an explicit refusal is the more honest engine behaviour.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 7, 'Of the five terms that set the matrix ceiling, which one does the engineer genuinely control on the day of the job?', '["The permeability, by choosing the acid strength.", "The drainage radius, because the ceiling responds strongly to how much rock the well is currently draining.", "The reservoir pressure, which can be raised or lowered by shutting the well in for long enough before pumping starts.", "The viscosity of the injected fluid, since gelling, foaming or adding a diverter lowers the ceiling in proportion."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Thickness also matters a great deal, but it is fixed in the completion design well before the pump truck arrives.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 8, 'Which viscosity belongs in the matrix ceiling calculation for an acid treatment?', '["The viscosity of the reservoir oil at reservoir conditions.", "The viscosity of the acid itself, close to water at about 0.001 Pa.s, since that is the fluid being injected.", "The fracturing fluid viscosity of 0.2 Pa.s, because the ceiling is defined at the fracturing pressure.", "An average of the acid and the reservoir fluid viscosities, weighted by the pore volumes each of them occupies."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Acid being close to water is why the computed ceiling is a near best case rather than a conservative one.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 9, 'A designer builds the ceiling for an acid job with the fracturing fluid viscosity of 0.2 Pa.s by mistake. What follows?', '["The ceiling is unaffected, since viscosity cancels out.", "The ceiling is understated by a factor of two hundred, falling to about 2.72e-06 m3/s on the published case.", "The ceiling rises by the same factor, which would send the designer to a rate the formation cannot possibly accept.", "The ceiling changes only slightly, because viscosity enters through a logarithm in the same way that the radii do."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Viscosity is linear in the denominator, so an error in it passes into the rate with no damping whatsoever.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 10, 'Hold every other input fixed and halve the drainage radius. Why does the ceiling barely move?', '["Because the drainage radius cancels against the wellbore radius.", "Because the drainage radius appears in the numerator as well, so the two appearances offset one another almost exactly.", "Because the skin term is recomputed from the new drainage radius, which restores the denominator to its previous value.", "The radii enter only through the logarithm of their ratio, so even large geometric changes buy little."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same weakness explains why underreaming a hole buys far less injectivity than the change in size suggests.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 11, 'Why is the well that most needs acid also the well that accepts acid most slowly?', '["Skin sits in the denominator, so the damage that restricts flow out of the well equally restricts flow in.", "Because damaged rock reacts with acid more slowly.", "Because a damaged well is usually completed with smaller tubing, which chokes the rate available at the perforations.", "Because the damaged zone holds a higher pore pressure than the rest of the reservoir, cutting the margin available."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ring of damage choking production is the very ring the acid has to be forced through on its way in.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 12, 'How does the published damaged skin of 8.481054145 compare with the geometric resistance term?', '["It is roughly one third of the geometric term.", "It is exactly equal to the geometric term, which is why the published ceiling comes out at half of the clean value.", "It is larger than the logarithm of 300 m over 0.108 m, so the damage more than doubles the denominator.", "It is smaller than the geometric term, so most of the resistance in a damaged well is still radial flow geometry."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That comparison is why the damaged well takes under half the rate the same well would accept once cleaned up.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 13, 'Why can a depleted well often accept acid faster than the same well when it was new?', '["Because depletion shrinks the drainage radius over time.", "Because depletion reduces the acid viscosity at reservoir conditions, which lifts the ceiling in direct proportion.", "Because the permeability improves as the pore pressure falls and the grain framework carries more of the load.", "Reservoir pressure falls with depletion while closure stays put, so the margin driving injection widens."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Neither closure nor reservoir pressure can be moved on the day of the job, but depletion moves one of them slowly.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 14, 'Module 3 planned 29.546905102 m3 of acid to take the front to 0.6 m. What does the published ceiling imply about that job?', '["Pumping alone runs to roughly fifteen hours at the maximum rate, with no margin and no allowance for shutdowns.", "It implies a pumping time of well under one hour.", "It implies that the volume must be cut in half, because no matrix job may exceed eight hours of continuous pumping.", "It implies nothing about time, since the ceiling is a pressure limit and the schedule is set by the acid reaction rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taking the front out to the damage at 0.9 m needs 67.71725584279905 m3, which is more than a day of pumping.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-matrix-ceiling', 15, 'What honest qualification does the engine attach to the single ceiling number it reports?', '["It is a laboratory value that needs a field correction factor.", "It is the rate the job should actually be pumped at, provided the acid is inhibited for the reservoir temperature.", "It is a steady state screening grade figure that ignores the early transient and assumes uniform acceptance.", "It is an upper bound on the volume rather than on the rate, so it constrains the tank count instead of the schedule."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reported value is also pessimistic in one respect, since the ceiling rises during the job as the acid removes skin.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'A junior engineer sizes the rate ceiling for a capstone well using the skin they expect once the acid has worked. What is wrong with that?', '["The ceiling describes the well as it stands before treatment, so the damage skin from the first step belongs there.", "Nothing is wrong, since the acid arrives only after the skin has fallen.", "Nothing is wrong, provided the figure used is an average of the values before and after the acid has been pumped away.", "Skin has no place in the rate equation at all, so any value taken from an earlier step is the wrong input here."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rate you can pump on the first barrel is the slowest of the job, and the well speeds up as the acid clears the ring.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'A trainee''s worksheet shows an acid front stopping short of the damaged radius yet reports a residual skin of zero. Which error does that indicate?', '["The pore volume factor has been left at unity.", "The wrong contrast has been carried into the Hawkins expression, so the untreated shell was priced at a ratio of one.", "Permeability has been treated as restored over rock the acid was never planned to reach.", "The residual has been quoted for the wellbore radius rather than for the front radius, which always collapses to nothing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine makes full removal a hard condition rather than a graded one, so money spent short of the damage earns no partial credit.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'A candidate''s carbonate step on the capstone well hands back a positive skin. What has gone wrong?', '["The pumped volume was entered in litres.", "The wormhole radius has come out smaller than the wellbore, which is an arithmetic slip rather than a physical result.", "The rock is too tight for wormholing, so the model has correctly reported that the channels never left the sandface.", "The pore volumes to breakthrough were taken from the laboratory at the wrong temperature, which flips the sign of the skin."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A wormhole network can only enlarge the flow area, so this model has no route to a positive value.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'An engineer runs the ceiling twice on the same well, at two different skins, and the larger skin returns the larger rate. What should they conclude?', '["The permeability thickness product was doubled between the two runs.", "The two runs used different fluids, so the viscosity rather than the skin is driving the difference in the answers.", "Nothing needs changing, because a more damaged well draws a wider pressure margin and therefore accepts fluid faster.", "Skin has been placed in the numerator or its sign has been reversed somewhere in the working."]'::jsonb, 3, 'This check costs nothing and needs no answer to hand, which is why it belongs beside the working rather than after it.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'Two colleagues argue over a capstone problem, and one insists the porosity of 0.18 from the published case still applies. Who is right?', '["The colleague, since porosity is a course constant.", "The objector, because the capstone states its own rock, interval, damage and fluids, and none of the published values carry across.", "Neither, because porosity plays no part in any of the five steps and so the argument has no bearing on the result.", "The colleague, provided the capstone interval is a sandstone, in which case the published rock properties may be reused."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Writing the stated conditions out as a named list before computing anything is the habit that stops this failure.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'A reviewer finds the wellbore radius typed wrongly at the top of a candidate''s capstone worksheet. How far does that single slip travel?', '["Into the Hawkins skin, the acid volume, the wormhole radius and the rate ceiling, since all four consume it.", "Into the Hawkins skin alone, since the other steps use radii of their own.", "Into the acid volume only, because that is the one step where an annular area has to be worked out from two radii.", "Nowhere beyond the first line, because each later step is given its own inputs by the statement of the problem."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Several capstone inputs appear in more than one calculation, which is why an early error is worth more than a late one.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'A candidate adds the carbonate pumped volume onto the sandstone planning volume and reports a single treatment. Why is that wrong?', '["Carbonate acid and sandstone acid cannot share a tank.", "The two volumes are quoted at different pore volume factors, so they would have to be rescaled before any addition made sense.", "The sandstone volume is a planning figure and the carbonate volume is measured, so only one of the two is an engineering number.", "The carbonate step is the other route on the same well, worked on the stated volume by itself."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rock frames the choice rather than the designer, so the two routes are alternatives set against each other and never stages.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'A geologist objects that a treatment reaching one metre into rock drained out to 300 m cannot possibly matter. How would you answer?', '["Reservoir maps are drawn at too coarse a scale to be trusted.", "The objection holds for oil wells but fails for gas wells, where the near wellbore velocity is high enough to dominate.", "Radial flow concentrates its pressure drop where the flow area is smallest, which is the first metre of rock.", "The treated metre is a large fraction of the drained volume once the interval height of the completion is taken into account."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is why a thin damaged ring can cost more in drawdown than a large volume of mediocre reservoir beyond it.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'A manager asks whether pushing the acid front half as far again makes the job half as expensive again. What is the honest reply?', '["Volume follows an area and so climbs far faster than that, while the skin it buys back moves only as a logarithm.", "Yes, because volume and radius move in direct proportion.", "No, because the extra acid is spent before it arrives, so the deeper front costs more and delivers nothing whatever.", "Yes for the volume, though the treatment time falls because a deeper front lowers the skin and lifts the rate ceiling."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two curves run through the whole tier, one logarithmic in radius and one quadratic in it, and every judgement sits on one of them.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'A recruiter asks what an Associate pass in this course lets a graduate do without supervision. Which claim is supportable?', '["Selecting a proppant and defending its conductivity at closure.", "Turning a contrast and a damaged radius into a skin, planning either acid route, and bounding the matrix pump rate.", "Designing a propped fracture from a pad fraction and a pump schedule, then reporting the pseudo skin the geometry earns.", "Reading a measured well test skin and splitting it into its damage, perforation, deviation and turbulence components."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reporting the residual honestly, with a statement of whether the front reached the damage, is part of the same claim.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'A colleague dismisses a net pressure of 2889735.9944400033 Pa as trivial beside a closure stress of 38131950.890444934 Pa. Why is that unsafe?', '["Net pressure is measured at surface and closure at depth.", "The two figures are quoted for different intervals of the published case, so comparing their sizes proves nothing at all.", "Closure stress is an estimate from the earth stress while net pressure is measured directly, so the smaller number is the firmer one.", "That small excess above closure is what holds the fracture open, so it decides the whole geometry."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sensitivity to inputs that look secondary is the reason fracture design demands more care than its arithmetic suggests.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'A designer picks KGD over PKN on the grounds that its average width of 0.0104637691458403 m is the larger of the two. What is missing?', '["The KGD figure quoted is a maximum width and not an average.", "Widths cannot be compared until both are corrected to the same half length, and the two figures were taken at different ones.", "The two models assume opposite directions of confinement, so the pick is a judgement about height containment.", "The wider model always places more proppant, so the choice should instead be settled on the mass the schedule can carry."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the published case at a half length of 150 m the two widths differ by a factor of 2.6055319672793997, so the pick is not cosmetic.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'A planner budgets pump time believing most of the injected fluid stays in the fracture. What does a fluid efficiency of 0.1728566723633056 tell them?', '["Most of what goes down the well leaks off into the rock, so the injection time is far longer than assumed.", "Efficiency describes the pumps rather than the fluid.", "Just over a sixth of the fluid leaks away, so the planner''s budget is sound and needs no revision before the job.", "Leakoff has been overstated by the model, since a value below a quarter is outside the range the material balance can resolve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That one figure also fixes the pad fraction, so an optimistic efficiency shortens the pad and risks a screenout.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'An Associate graduate asks whether they may begin the Professional tier straight away. What does the tier assume they can already state?', '["How closure stress is read from a leakoff test.", "How the proppant pack keeps its conductivity once the fracture has closed and the stress is carried by the grains.", "What a skin is, why it costs money, and why a damaged well is slow to accept treatment.", "How the pad and the proppant ramp are apportioned across a pump schedule, since the tier opens with that arithmetic."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those three came from the damage, the cost and the ceiling modules, which is why the tiers are ordered as they are.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'A worksheet takes the square of the acid front radius, multiplies by height and porosity, and subtracts nothing for the hole itself. What follows?', '["The volume is unaffected, since the hole holds no pore space.", "The volume comes out too large, because the pore space of the open wellbore has been charged to the acid.", "The volume comes out too small, because the annular area is measured outward from the front rather than from the wellbore.", "The volume is right but the residual skin is wrong, since the omitted term belongs to the Hawkins expression instead."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The wellbore term weighs most at small fronts, which is why the volume table climbs slightly faster than a pure square would.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Two underperforming wells are tested. The first returns a small skin in 1 mD rock, the second a large skin in 200 mD rock. Which one is the acid candidate?', '["The second, because a large skin sitting over good permeability is manufactured damage that acid can give back.", "The first, since a low skin proves the acid will travel far.", "Both of them, because any well producing below the reservoir map carries near wellbore plugging that a matrix treatment removes.", "Neither, since the choice of treatment follows the rock chemistry rather than anything a pressure transient test reports."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Skin and permeability fall out of the same test, which is why one survey separates the damaged well from the merely tight one.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'A client asks for a sandstone matrix job that finishes at a negative skin. What is the honest reply?', '["Negative skin simply needs a larger acid volume than planned.", "It is achievable once the front is pushed past the damaged radius, because acid pumped after that point keeps improving the well.", "Restoration cannot better the rock as it was drilled, so zero is the floor and only wormholes or a fracture pass it.", "Only if hydrofluoric blends are used, since those dissolve the quartz framework itself and enlarge the flow area."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine reports removal as a flag, and once it flips the skin sits at zero however much more acid is pumped.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Management wants the app to report the extra barrels a day a treatment will earn. What can it supply instead?', '["A decline curve for the treated well over its first year.", "A skin and a geometry, which are dimensionless statements about pressure drop rather than any rate of oil.", "A production forecast, since the rate equation inside the matrix ceiling already turns a pressure margin into a flowing rate.", "Nothing whatever, because the engine holds no reservoir properties of any kind once the skin has been returned."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Turning a skin into a rate needs a reservoir model with fluid properties and a drawdown, and that sits outside this course.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'An input set drives the drainage logarithm plus the skin below zero. Why does the rate model stop rather than return a very large number?', '["Because the pump would then exceed the fracturing pressure.", "Because the drainage radius must exceed the wellbore radius, and a negative sum shows the two were entered the wrong way round.", "Because a negative skin cannot arise in a well that has never been fractured or acidised.", "A negative denominator is nonsense rather than a fast well, so the engine refuses instead of reporting the arithmetic."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same discipline shows in the other guards, where a contrast below 1 and a front inside the hole are both thrown out.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Acid clears every unit of damage from a well in 1 mD rock and the well still disappoints. What has been learnt?', '["The acid was probably placed in the wrong interval.", "The damage ran deeper than the front reached, so a second and larger job on the same design ought to be scheduled.", "The manufactured skin has gone and tight rock is what remains, which no near wellbore treatment can alter.", "The permeability contrast was estimated too high, meaning the skin was never worth the money spent on treating it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Damage and low permeability look identical at surface, and only the pair of numbers from a well test separates them.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Somebody asks this engine for a proppant ramp and a propped conductivity. Where do those calculations sit?', '["In the fracture family, which this tier reads about but does not compute, leaving that work to the tiers above.", "In the matrix family, alongside the sandstone planning volume.", "Nowhere at all, because proppant settling and proppant transport are both excluded, so nothing in the engine deals with a pack.", "In the matrix family, since the rate ceiling already fixes how much proppant the formation will accept."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Height is an input on the fracture side and stays fixed, so even there the engine will not say when a fracture leaves its zone.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'A vendor claims a blend with twice the dissolving power should halve the planned job. Where does that argument fail?', '["It fails because acid strength is capped by tubular corrosion.", "It holds, since the pore volume factor of 1.5 is precisely the allowance the engine makes for the dissolving power of a blend.", "It holds in carbonate but not in sandstone, where the framework is quartz and hydrochloric acid attacks very little.", "No chemistry enters the sandstone rule, so strength has no route into a volume built from pore space alone."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mineralogy, preflush design and spending rate are all handed back to the laboratory by this engine.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Two wells each test near a skin of 5. One is diagnosed as a contrast of 5 reaching 0.4 m, the other as a contrast of 3 reaching 0.9 m. Which is cheaper to clear?', '["The deeper one, because milder damage dissolves more readily.", "The shallower one, since the front need only reach 0.4 m and volume follows the square of that radius.", "Neither is cheaper, because the two wells carry a similar skin and the planning volume is driven by the skin being removed.", "The deeper one, since the pore volume factor falls away as the treated annulus grows larger."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well test hands over the skin and never the pair behind it, which is why the treatment design needs the radius separately.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The damage ring is cleared exactly as designed, yet the retest still reports a positive skin. What should the client be told?', '["A measured skin is a sum, and the perforation and turbulence terms inside it were never acid targets.", "The acid was spent before it ever left the tubing.", "The job failed, because a damage ring that has genuinely been removed always brings the measured skin back to zero.", "The test was run too early, and the same well will read zero once it has cleaned up over a few weeks of flow."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Splitting a measured skin before sizing a job is what stops a good treatment being written up as a failure.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'A well is tested twice at different flow rates and returns two different skins. Which component of the measured value explains that?', '["Partial penetration, which shifts with the applied drawdown.", "Formation damage, since the faster test drags more fines into the pore throats and deepens the damaged ring as it runs.", "Turbulence, the rate dependent term, which grows with flow rate instead of staying put as the damage ring does.", "Well deviation, which contributes a negative term that scales with the rate through the completion."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hawkins knows nothing about how fast a well flows, so only part of a measured skin was ever its to explain.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'The same damage skin of 8.481054145 turns up in a 1 mD well and in a 1000 mD well. Which question does that leave unanswered?', '["How thick the damaged ring around the hole actually is.", "How severe the damage is, because the contrast cancels out of the expression as soon as the two radii have been fixed.", "Whether the skin is positive or negative, which needs an absolute permeability before it can be settled.", "How good the reservoir is, since the formula carries only a ratio and never an absolute permeability value."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That independence is exactly why a skin can be compared across wells, fields and fluids without any conversion.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'An estimate of the permeability contrast is revised by one unit on the eve of a job. How far does that revision travel?', '["It changes nothing until the front radius is revised too.", "It shifts the skin by a fixed step and moves the rate ceiling with it, while the planning volume does not budge.", "It changes the acid volume in proportion, because the pore volume of the treated annulus is scaled by the damage contrast.", "It touches only the residual skin reported after treatment, since the damage before the job had already been measured."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the published damaged radius every unit of contrast is worth 2.120263536200091 of skin, wherever you sit on the scale.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Logs show the invaded zone is patchy and reaches further on one side of the hole. What does Hawkins assume in its place?', '["A gradual permeability taper running away from the wellbore.", "A set of separate invaded lobes, each carrying its own contrast, which the formula averages before returning one skin.", "A clean cylindrical ring of uniformly degraded rock, with untouched formation everywhere beyond its edge.", "That the damage is thickest at the perforations and largely absent in the rock between them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The model is one line of algebra from 1956, and its tidiness is the price of turning a description into a number.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Fresh core work halves the damaged permeability while both radii stand. What becomes of the damage skin?', '["The contrast doubles and the skin more than doubles, because the subtracted 1 counts for less at a larger contrast.", "It doubles exactly, since skin is linear in the contrast.", "It is unchanged, because the formula uses only the ratio of the two permeabilities and that ratio was fixed by the radii.", "It falls, since a lower damaged permeability slows further invasion and keeps the ring thin."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The linear step in the sweep is measured from a contrast of 1, which is where the formula places a clean ring.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Full removal on the published well asks for 67.71725584279905 m3. Pumped at the ceiling the damaged rock allows, what does the schedule look like?', '["About an hour, since the ceiling is a maximum rate.", "It cannot be scheduled at all, because the ceiling is quoted at the clean skin and the damaged well will take no fluid.", "Roughly four hours, which matches the ratio between the two published acid volumes.", "More than a day of continuous pumping at the maximum rate, with no shutdowns and no margin built in."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ceiling itself lifts as acid clears the near wellbore, so the opening barrels are the slowest of the whole job.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Somebody proposes 8 m3 for the published sandstone interval, the volume the carbonate case pumps. What is the objection?', '["Sandstone acid cannot be pumped in volumes below 10 m3.", "That barely carries the front past 0.3 m, leaving most of the damaged annulus at its reduced permeability.", "None, because 8 m3 in the carbonate case reached 0.391324751 m and the same volume reaches the same radius in sandstone.", "That the pore volume factor would have to be lifted well above 1.5 to make so small a job legitimate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reaching 0.3 m in this interval takes 6.6446946570134715 m3 and leaves a residual skin of 4.394449154672438.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'A budget cut pulls the planned front back from 0.9 m to 0.6 m on the published well. What is saved and what is surrendered?', '["Nothing is surrendered, since both fronts clear the damage.", "The acid falls by a third and the residual skin rises in proportion, since skin and volume both follow the front radius.", "The acid falls from 67.72 m3 to 29.55 m3, and the damage is no longer removed, leaving a residual skin of 1.62.", "The volume halves and the removal flag stays true, because the damaged shell is very thin at that radius."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A job that stops short earns no partial credit in proportion to the money spent, and the engine reports removal as false.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'To trim cost, a designer sets the pore volume factor to 1. What has quietly been assumed?', '["Perfect piston displacement of every pore in the treated annulus, which no real interval ever delivers.", "That the acid is twice as strong as the published blend.", "That the damage is mild, since the factor is the engine''s way of scaling a planned job to the measured permeability contrast.", "That the interval is shorter than the 100 m the published case treats."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fingering into permeable streaks, gravity override and spending on the way out are the three reasons the factor sits above 1.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'In a hot well the designed volume goes away at the designed rate and dead acid arrives at the sandface. Which silence in the model does that expose?', '["There is no porosity term, so the pore space is guessed at.", "There is no allowance for the wellbore radius being subtracted, so the annular volume is overstated in a hot well.", "There is no permeability in the volume rule, which is why the acid could not reach the damaged rock.", "There is no time, no temperature and no reaction rate in it, so live acid penetration is never modelled."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The distance live acid travels is a race between the flow rate and the reaction rate, and this engine runs neither clock.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Spending 29.55 m3 of the 67.72 m3 that full removal costs takes the well from 8.48 to 1.62. How is that best described?', '["A failed job, since the removal flag never flipped at all.", "A linear return, because the skin recovered and the acid pumped stand in the same proportion right across the sweep.", "Most of the skin recovered for well under half the acid, though the engine still reports the damage as present.", "An unusually poor return, since two thirds of the damaged radius was reached for nearly half the full volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The last 1.62 of skin costs more than the entire job that came before it, which is the square law working against you.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'On the eve of pumping a petrophysicist revises the permeability of the damaged ring. Which planned numbers move?', '["Only the planning volume, which scales with permeability.", "The damage skin and the rate ceiling both move, while the planning volume stays exactly where it was.", "All of them, because permeability enters the annular pore volume, the damage skin and the steady state rate alike.", "None of them, since the engine works from a measured skin rather than from any permeability value at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sandstone volume depends on geometry and porosity alone, which is why it can be written before the logs are read.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Pump the published carbonate job of 8 m3 into the sandstone interval instead. What happens there?', '["It restores rock rather than removing it, and a front barely past 0.3 m leaves the damage largely in place.", "It cuts wormholes of the same 0.391324751 m radius.", "It returns a negative skin of similar size, because the volumetric balance behind both routes is the identical expression.", "It removes the damage, since 8 m3 exceeds the pore volume of the annulus out to the damaged radius."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hydrochloric acid does almost nothing to a quartz framework, so nothing in a sandstone is dissolved into a channel.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'The laboratory report is missing, so a carbonate design assumes a breakthrough count of 1. The rock demands four times that. What is delivered?', '["The designed radius, reached more slowly than planned.", "A radius four times smaller than planned, since the wormhole radius is inversely proportional to the breakthrough count.", "The radius a quarter of the volume would have bought, so 8 m3 performs like 2 m3 at 0.2168680823254826 m.", "The designed radius but a positive skin, because the channels stop short of the wall of the hole."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pumped volume and breakthrough count appear only as a quotient, so an optimistic laboratory number and underpumping are one mistake.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Asked to double the negative skin the published carbonate job returns, what is the reply?', '["It takes twice the acid, since skin follows pumped volume.", "It cannot be answered at all, because the engine returns only a wormhole radius and never converts that radius into a skin.", "It takes about four times the acid, matching the square root sitting in the radius expression.", "Each doubling of acid buys a fixed decrement, and four times the volume only reaches -1.9511423998762085."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The decrement settles towards half the natural logarithm of two, so the cost of the next unit of skin doubles each time.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'A spreadsheet is told to optimise the carbonate volume and hands back the largest number it is allowed. Why was the request unfair?', '["The engine caps the pumped volume at 32 m3 in any case.", "The skin improves for ever inside this model, so the stopping point has to come from a cost curve outside it.", "The optimum is an injection rate rather than a volume, and the engine works that rate out from the breakthrough count.", "The wormhole radius reaches a plateau, so the spreadsheet ought to have stopped where that curve flattened."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Carbonate jobs cluster at modest volumes for exactly that reason, because the first few cubic metres do most of the work.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'A reviewer reads the returned 0.391324751 m as how far the channels actually run. Correct them.', '["It is the radius of an equivalent fully stimulated cylinder, which is the fiction that lets a skin be written down.", "It is the radius of the largest single channel cut.", "It is the outer edge of the damaged ring after treatment, measured from the centre of the hole rather than from its wall.", "It is the average length of the channels, weighted by how much acid each of them took."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Real wormholes are a sparse branching network with untouched rock between them, which no single radius describes.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'A damaged carbonate is treated and a worksheet adds the Hawkins skin to the wormhole skin for one treated value. What does the tier say?', '["The sum is right, since skins add inside the flow equation.", "The sum is correct only where the wormhole radius exceeds the damaged radius, and in the published case it comfortably does.", "The two results are kept apart, and the engine will not hand back a single treated skin for such a well.", "The damage skin should be subtracted instead, since the channels bypass the damaged shell entirely."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Physically such a well ought to end better than undamaged, but the engine declines to combine the two mechanisms.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Only half the interval is opened and the same carbonate volume goes away. Which way does the wormhole radius move?', '["Downward, in direct proportion to the open height.", "Upward, because the same acid now has to fill the pore space of a shorter cylinder before breaking through.", "It stays exactly where it was, since interval height cancels between the pumped volume and the pore volumes to breakthrough.", "Downward, since a shorter interval reduces the number of channels the instability is able to start."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Height sits under the square root beside porosity and the breakthrough count, so halving it lifts the radius by a modest amount.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'A viscous diverter raises the pumping fluid viscosity tenfold. What follows for the ceiling and for the clock?', '["The ceiling is unaffected, since viscosity cancels out.", "The ceiling rises, because a thicker fluid carries more pressure to the sandface and widens the margin available for injection.", "The ceiling falls with the square of the viscosity, so the treatment becomes impractical.", "The ceiling falls in direct proportion, and the same volume then takes ten times as long to place."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Viscosity is the single term in that equation the engineer genuinely owns on the day of the job.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Water injection returns a depleted field towards its original pressure. What does that do to the matrix ceiling?', '["It narrows the margin between fracturing and reservoir pressure, so the ceiling falls and the job runs longer.", "It lifts the ceiling, since the well is livelier than before.", "It leaves the ceiling untouched, because the fracturing pressure is set by earth stress and follows the reservoir upward.", "It raises the fracturing pressure by the same amount, which keeps the ceiling exactly where it stood."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That margin is the entire budget a matrix job has, and it is set by the reservoir and the earth rather than by surface.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'During the first minutes of injection a well accepts more than the computed ceiling without breaking down. Is the number wrong?', '["Yes, the fracturing pressure has been set far too low.", "Yes, since the equation assumes the whole of the interval accepts fluid uniformly, and no real interval has ever done that.", "No, it is a steady state figure that ignores the opening transient, and it is labelled screening grade.", "No, because the well is quietly fracturing and will show that on the pressure record a little later."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Most designers pump below the limit anyway, since diversion works better there and margin on a computed pressure is cheap.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Half of the 100 m interval is left behind casing. What has that done to the ceiling, and when was it decided?', '["It doubles the ceiling, since less rock now resists flow.", "It halves the ceiling, and the decision belongs to the completion design rather than to the pump truck.", "It leaves the ceiling alone, because thickness cancels against the pore volume the acid has to fill in that interval.", "It halves the ceiling, but the choice can be reversed on the day by opening a few more perforations."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Permeability and thickness sit in the numerator as a product, so either of them doubles the rate when it doubles.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Underreaming the hole is offered as a way to lift injectivity on a damaged well. How does that proposal read?', '["Poorly, since the radius ratio enters through a logarithm and a large geometric change buys very little rate.", "Well, because the ceiling scales with the radius of the hole.", "Well, since enlarging the hole removes the damaged ring itself and takes the skin term out of the denominator entirely.", "Poorly, because a wider hole raises the pressure margin the job has to work against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The same flatness shows on the damage side, where taking the damaged radius from 0.9 m to 3 m adds under five units of skin.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Set beside the published contrast of 5, a well at a contrast of 12 takes how long to place the same acid?', '["The same time, because volume does not depend on skin.", "Rather less time, because the more damaged well carries the larger pressure margin and therefore accepts fluid faster.", "Around a third longer, which matches the ratio of the two skins in the Hawkins sweep.", "Roughly twice as long, since its ceiling drops to about a quarter of the clean rate instead of to under half."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Severe damage is not a slightly slower job, and pump spread, tanks and inhibitor life all follow the hours.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Chemically a treatment is cheap, but it needs more than a day of continuous pumping on a shut in well. What should weigh in the decision?', '["Nothing, because acid cost is the only real cost line here.", "Only the acid strength, since a stronger blend shortens the job without changing the volume that has to be placed.", "The hours themselves, since pump spread, tanks and crew time can push the job towards the fracture route.", "The drainage radius, because a longer job drains a wider area and improves the economics of the well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A treatment that looks cheap on chemical volume can be unaffordable on time, and that has decided real jobs.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Before anything else on the capstone well, which quantity has to be settled, and why does it come first?', '["The planning volume, since it fixes the job ticket.", "The damage skin from the ratio and the radius, because the rate ceiling is then taken at that very skin.", "The wormhole radius, because the carbonate route continues the sandstone job and needs its treated front as a start.", "The residual skin, since every later step is measured against what the treatment has left behind."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Several inputs appear in more than one calculation, so listing the stated conditions before computing anything saves rework.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Put the four near wellbore radii of the published well in order, working outward from the hole.', '["Wellbore 0.108 m, then the wormhole radius 0.391324751 m, then the acid front 0.6 m, then damage at 0.9 m.", "Damage 0.9 m, front 0.6 m, then wellbore 0.108 m.", "Wellbore 0.108 m, then the acid front at 0.6 m, then the wormhole radius, and the damaged radius last of all.", "Wormhole 0.391324751 m, wellbore 0.108 m, damage 0.9 m, acid front 0.6 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The whole tier argues about the first metre of rock inside a reservoir that drains out to 300 m.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'The tier says two curve shapes keep coming back. Which pair are they, and what do they cost you?', '["Pressure through a square root and rate through a line.", "Skin through an exponential and volume through a cube, which is why deep treatments are dearer than shallow ones.", "Permeability through a logarithm and porosity through a square, which is why both are measured with such care.", "Radius through a logarithm, which gives poor returns, and volume through an area, which makes depth expensive."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every judgement in the Associate tier is one of those two curves read in one direction or the other.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Porosity is typed wrongly at the top of a capstone worksheet. Which results carry the error onward?', '["Every result, since porosity enters all of the steps.", "Only the rate ceiling, because porosity decides how much fluid the pore system will accept in a given period of time.", "The planning volume and the wormhole radius, while the damage skin and the rate ceiling stand unaffected.", "Only the damage skin, since Hawkins works on the pore space inside the damaged ring."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A wrong wellbore radius is worse still, because that one appears in all four of the tier''s calculations.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'A report gives a residual skin and stops there. What has the writer left out?', '["The pore volume factor used in building the volume.", "Whether the front reached the damaged radius, without which no reader can tell if removal was being claimed.", "The measured skin from the well test, which the residual has to be subtracted from before the number means anything.", "The injection rate, since a residual skin is valid only at the rate it was computed for."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A residual of zero from a front that stopped short means rock the acid never reached has been counted as restored.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'Which neighbouring courses hand this one the inputs it consumes?', '["Geomechanics for the stress that sets closure, and Petrophysics for permeability, porosity and thickness.", "Drilling Fluids for the acid blend and for its strength.", "Production Chemistry for the reaction kinetics and Well Testing for the pore volumes to breakthrough used in carbonate.", "Perforating and Sand Control, which supplies the damaged radius the Hawkins step works from."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Perforating and Sand Control answers the same connection problem geometrically rather than chemically or hydraulically.'),
  ('stimulation', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'What question does the next tier take up that this one deliberately left closed?', '["How acid strength is chosen for a given mineralogy.", "How a measured skin ought to be split into its damage, perforation and turbulence parts before any job is sized.", "How the pore volumes to breakthrough might be predicted without any laboratory work.", "What the rock does once the pressure is carried above closure on purpose and a fracture is grown."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Net pressure, the two width models and the material balance are the three ideas waiting at the Professional tier.');
